 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear in the number of bounces $b$ and the number of objects $O$ per bounce. Total complexity $O(b \cdot |O|)$.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary space per ray, excluding the scene structure $O$.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
 *   **Performance:** Reduces shader inlining overhead by consolidating calls. In one implementation, inlining 2 walks per level (vs 6 in recursive) held fewer registers, improving performance [S2, unofficial].
 *   **Strengths:** Compatible with GPU languages lacking recursion; reduced register pressure; easier to maintain than multiple hardcoded bounce functions [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Fixed maximum bounce depth; less flexible than recursive for varying bounce counts per ray.
-*   **When to Use:** GPU implementations of ray tracing where recursion is unsupported or problematic.
-*   **When Not to Use:** CPU implementations where recursion is efficient and flexible; scenes requiring highly variable bounce depths.
